5. Extend controller handling for two independent players

This is the most important technical requirement.

Alva already supports connecting multiple controllers, but the gameplay input system currently reads only Player 1’s controller.

Supporting multiple connected controllers is not the same as supporting multiple human players.

You must implement true independent input processing.

Controller assignments

Local Play requires:

* Player 1 → Controller A
* Player 2 → Controller B

The controllers must be physically distinct devices.

Use the existing ControllerManager’s device identity and ordering system.

Preserve the existing manually selected Player 1 controller when possible.

Assign Player 2 to the next eligible active controller that is not Player 1’s controller.

Keep assignments stable throughout a Local Play session.

If a third or fourth controller is connected, it must not automatically interfere with either player’s inputs.

If controller assignments are exposed in Controller Connections, clearly label which device belongs to Player 1 and which belongs to Player 2.

Prevent assigning the same device to both players.

Separate input state

Each player needs their own:

* Held buttons.
* Newly pressed buttons.
* Released buttons.
* Analog stick axes.
* Trigger states.
* Controller binding resolver.
* Press-edge handling.
* Gameplay snapshot.
* Input buffering.
* Reset and flush behavior.

Never merge both controllers into the same gameplay snapshot.

For example, Controller 1 pressing Jump must not cause Player 2 to jump.

Controller 2 pressing Attack must not cause Player 1 to attack.

Both players must be able to press the same action at the same time and have both actions execute.

The code should support two independent input pipelines without unnecessarily duplicating the entire InputManager implementation.

A per-player resolver and input-state abstraction is preferable.

Maintain backwards compatibility with the existing single-player input APIs wherever possible.

Fighter-specific controller profiles

Alva already gives each fighter a recommended controller layout and supports customized per-fighter bindings.

Both players must use the appropriate controls for their own selected fighters.

For example:

* Player 1 chooses Fighter #0001.
* Player 2 chooses Fighter #0002.
* Controller 1 uses Fighter #0001’s resolved bindings.
* Controller 2 uses Fighter #0002’s resolved bindings.

They must not share a single fighter profile accidentally.

If both players select the same fighter, they may share the same profile definition but must still use independent input state and binding resolution.

Saved custom controller layouts must remain compatible.

All existing universal controls must work independently for both players, including:

* Movement
* Jump and air jumps
* Super Jump
* Dash and air dash
* Fast fall and platform drop
* Attacks and extra attacks
* Shield and Deflect
* Character-specific special moves
* Directional stick inputs
* Button modifiers
* Hold-based bindings

Do not simplify or remove any fighter’s unique mechanics to make multiplayer work.

Controller-only gameplay

Local Play must never use keyboard or touch controls for either fighter.

Do not fall back to:

* Keyboard for Player 1
* Keyboard for Player 2
* One controller plus keyboard
* One controller plus touch
* Two players sharing one controller

The Home Screen may still be clicked normally to enter Local Play when the controller requirements are satisfied, but local fighter selection, stage confirmation, and combat should use their assigned controllers.

The existing keyboard, touch, and controller behavior in other game modes must remain unchanged.

Controller menu navigation

The current menu navigator is built around Player 1.

Extend it carefully so the active local setup screen can determine which controller owns menu navigation.

Required ownership:

* Select local mode: Player 1
* Player 1 fighter selection: Player 1
* Player 2 fighter selection: Player 2
* Local stage selection: Player 1

Preserve D-pad navigation, analog cursor behavior, confirmation, and Back commands.

Only the appropriate assigned controller should change or confirm a local selection.

Do not permanently change the application’s Player 1 assignment merely to let Player 2 navigate a menu.

Outside Local Play, preserve the existing Player 1 menu navigation behavior.

Pause behavior

During Local Battle, either player’s controller must be able to pause.

Use Alva’s existing Options / Menu input mapping.

The first pause request opens the shared pause interface.

Both fighters must stop receiving gameplay input while the match is paused.

A simultaneous pause press must not open multiple dialogs or immediately close the menu.

Closing the pause menu must flush stale inputs for both players.

When resuming, no held menu button should accidentally trigger an attack.

