13. Recommended implementation architecture

Follow the repository’s established structure.

New screens

Introduce dedicated Local Play screens or configurable subclasses:

* local-mode
* local-character-p1
* local-character-p2
* local-map

Register them through App.start() and add the corresponding sections to index.html.

Reuse existing screen classes and visual components wherever practical.

Selection state

Keep local choices under a separate namespace, such as app.selection.local.

Do not conflate local Player 2 with cpuCharacterId.

Battle mode

Extend the existing battle mode model with local.

Ensure the Battle Screen correctly routes Local Play to the two-human Battle configuration.

Update all mode-specific routing tables, including those for:

* Change stage.
* Change fighters.
* Pause-screen mode labels.
* Results.
* Victory announcements.

Do not allow an unknown mode string to silently turn a requested Local Play match into normal Quick Battle.

Input architecture

Introduce separate controller input contexts and binding resolvers for the two players.

Keep the existing InputManager behavior compatible for ordinary Play.

Avoid duplicating shared code or attaching unnecessary extra global event listeners.

A clear per-player interface is preferable to many scattered conditions checking for Local Play.

Ability HUD

Allow AbilityHUD to receive an explicit player/controller context.

Instantiate and bind two HUDs in Local Battle.

Keep one HUD in normal Play and none in Watch Mode.

Styling

Use existing CSS files and introduce narrowly scoped local-mode selectors.

Use a shared color system for the red and blue identities.

Prevent local-specific colors or dual-HUD positioning from leaking into normal battle modes.

Cleanup

When navigating away, ensure:

* Local input resources are released.
* Both HUDs are hidden and unbound.
* Event listeners are removed where appropriate.
* Paused state is cleared.
* The connection-warning UI is closed.
* The usual controller navigation system is restored.

