16. Final implementation principles

Do not create a separate miniature multiplayer game. Build Local Play into Alva itself.

Reuse the game’s existing systems wherever possible.

Prioritize correct two-player gameplay over superficial multiplayer UI.

In particular, avoid these mistakes:

* Merely relabeling the CPU as Player 2.
* Reading both physical controllers through the same input resolver.
* Showing identical input glyphs for both players when their controller types differ.
* Giving both players the same ability HUD state.
* Accidentally routing Local Play through the existing CPU setup.
* Allowing a disconnected controller to silently turn into AI control.
* Breaking single-player input or menu navigation.
* Making red/blue identification visible only in the top HUD but not on the actual fighters.
* Showing two HUDs without checking their responsiveness.
* Adding English-only strings.
* Creating an entirely different visual design for Local Play.

Maintain the existing code conventions, avoid unnecessary dependencies, and preserve the game’s established identity.

17. Expected deliverables

Implement the feature completely in the repository.

After implementation, provide:

1. A summary of the actual changes.
2. A list of the important modified and newly created files.
3. An explanation of the two-controller input architecture.
4. Confirmation of how Player 1 and Player 2 are assigned.
5. Confirmation of the new Local Play screen flow.
6. Confirmation that both move HUDs work independently.
7. Automated test results.
8. Any limitations that genuinely remain.

Do not stop after creating the Home button or selection screens. The task is finished only when two real players can select their fighters, choose a stage, enter battle, fight simultaneously using two controllers, see their own move HUDs, finish the match, and rematch successfully.

The finished experience should feel like Local Play was always intended to be part of Alva.
